The "vfzmat" supplement has code that generates F- and Z-matrices and Greek coefficients, 
given an input data file of Fmatrix entries at a set of specified input angles. It is not very efficient,
so I have written some code to speed things up. The basic "Master" routine is now divided into
two parts :-

1, the "premaster" subroutine which calculates all necessary geometrical information
  and all spherical functions and which is called first, outside of any hyperspectral loop;

2, the "postmaster" subroutine which delivers the desired F/Z/Greekmat output, using the
   precalculated  geometry/GSFs, and which is designed to be used inside a spectral loop.

To save memory, I used  allocatable arrays for  quadrature-related goemetry and spherical functions,
and used dynmaical memeory where possible.

The test compares thw two ways of doing things, using a real data set of ice habitat F-matrix.
All runs were done for 50 hyperspectral points, with the number of stokes elements (NSTOKES)
and discrete-ordinates (NSTREAMS) and quadratures (NQUADS) controlled with a short input file
(TOPLEVEL.inp). The  quadrature number is hard-wired parameter statement in the old master routine,
and this must be changed by hand.

I tried NSTOKES = 1/3/4 and NSTREAMS = 16/24/48, and NQuads = 2000/5000. In all cases, the
F-matrix and Greekmat results are identical (old and new). There's a small file with some
timing results for 50 points. Speedups are quite impressive.

